Durarara!! AU
Chapter 21 – Part 1
Morning at Raira did not look like a crisis.
That was what made it worse.
Curtis arrived on time and unrested, long black braids neat against his uniform, mismatched eyes half-lidded in the particular way of a man who had not slept and had decided that fact was nobody’s business. Rio found him first in the flow of the corridor and fell into step with an ease that had not been there a week ago. Anri saw them from farther down the hall and felt the now-familiar double pull in her chest: relief that he was actually present, and the leftover bruise of yesterday’s fight, the stare, the hours of unanswered calls, the second phone call that had patched something without fully repairing it. She was wearing her own shoes. By third period, she would not be.
Mikado overslept.
He woke to the kind of lateness that already felt symbolic, dragged himself through the shortest possible version of getting ready, and hit the academy gates with his breath still uneven. He had wanted the morning to restore the old shape of things: Masaomi talking too much, Anri quiet and near, himself in the useful middle of it. Instead he opened the classroom door on a map that had already moved.
Curtis was there.
Rio was angled toward him like the air between them had become familiar.
Anri sat carefully, not cold, not open, watching Curtis in brief controlled glances that Mikado was not supposed to notice and noticed anyway.
Masaomi was already in his seat with a smile that looked ordinary until you saw how hard it was working.
Mikado crossed to his desk late, apologizing to the teacher, and felt the displacement before he had language for it. He had not fallen behind in class. He had fallen behind in the room.
Masaomi leaned over as soon as it was safe.
“Rough morning, prince of tardiness? You look like the city personally snubbed you.”
The joke landed just enough to keep other people from looking. It did not land enough to fix Mikado’s face. Masaomi saw that too. His eyes flicked once toward Curtis, then Anri, then back, filing the geometry with the grim efficiency of someone who had already spent too many days trying to keep his friend out of worse weather.
Curtis did not do anything dramatic.
That was the other problem. He answered when spoken to. He did not hold court. He looked like a transfer student running on too little sleep and too much leftover city. And still the room bent around him. Rio’s ease. Anri’s caution. The faint public residue of a street-fight video some classmates had definitely already seen and were pretending they had not. Mikado sat inside all of it with a late arrival and no role.
Then Anri’s shoes were gone.
It happened in the ordinary way lost things happened at Raira: one moment they were where they were supposed to be, the next they were not. Anri noticed at the changeover, went still, and checked the same places twice as if repetition might restore them. A few classmates offered useless sympathy. Someone laughed too lightly. Anri’s face tightened in that quiet way that meant she was trying not to become a scene.
Mikado stood up so fast his chair clicked.
“I’ll help look.”
It came out immediate, almost grateful for the task. A missing pair of shoes was solvable. A missing pair of shoes was kind. A missing pair of shoes was a way back into the center of a morning that had already started without him.
Anri hesitated, then nodded.
“…Thank you, Ryuugamine-kun.”
From two seats over, Masaomi watched Mikado seize the small emergency like a life raft and felt the joke die in his throat. Across the room, Curtis noticed the commotion only after it had already acquired a search party. He looked at Anri’s stockinged feet, then at Mikado already in motion, and filed the problem as practical rather than symbolic.
He did not yet know he would be the one to replace what Mikado was about to spend the day trying to recover.
Mikado did not know that either.
He only knew the room had changed, the shoes were gone, and if he could find them, maybe the day would still let him matter in the old way.
Curtis lasted through the next stretch of class before the practical problem overtook the symbolic one.
Anri was still in school with no shoes. Mikado was still treating the absence like a quest that would restore the morning if he just searched hard enough. Masaomi was still watching both of them with the tight cheer of a man trying to keep a crack from becoming a canyon. None of that changed the floor-level fact: Anri could not spend the rest of the day, or the walk home, in ruined absence.
Curtis looked at the clock. Then at Anri’s feet. Then at the door.
He stood.
The teacher barely had time to register the movement before Curtis was already murmuring an excuse — something about an urgent errand, said flatly enough to discourage follow-up — and slipping into the corridor. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he cut through the flow of students with the same unshowy speed he used when a situation had finished being discussed and needed solving. He did not tell Mikado. He did not make a speech to Anri. He did not ask Rio to cover. He just left, because the missing pair had already consumed enough of the room’s attention and none of that attention was putting anything wearable back on her feet.
Outside the gates, Ikebukuro’s daytime noise met him like a second schedule. He checked sizes from memory and from the brief look he’d gotten when the commotion started, then headed for the nearest place that would sell something clean, simple, and not loud enough to turn a kindness into a scene. No limited colorway. No statement pair. Just dark, understated Nikes in the right size, the kind of shoe a person could actually walk in without becoming a rumor on top of the rumor she was already standing next to.
The transaction took minutes.
Box under his arm, receipt folded away, Curtis started back toward Raira at a faster clip than he had left it. He did not think of himself as interrupting Mikado’s search. He thought of himself as closing a gap the search might not close in time. If the old pair turned up ruined, she would still need something on her feet. If they turned up intact, she would have a spare. Either way, the problem stopped being theater.
What he did not account for — because he was solving, not staging — was the image he was about to walk into:
Mikado returning with whatever was left of the old pair.
Anri standing there with no good place to put her gratitude.
And Curtis arriving with a replacement already in hand, as if finding the lost thing and ending the loss were never the same job to begin with.
He hit the academy steps with the box under his arm and the unrested night still in his eyes, unaware that the small errand had already become the next fracture in the room.
Mikado found them behind the east-side outdoor landing, half-kicked under the edge of a planter like they had been treated as trash the moment they stopped being useful.
He recognized the pair at once and felt the flash of victory before the condition of them caught up. Soaked. One heel collapsed. A seam split wide enough that no amount of optimism would make them wearable again. They were the lost thing he had spent the day hunting, restored to him in the most useless possible form.
He picked them up anyway.
By the time he got back inside, the box was already in Curtis’s hands.
The scene arranged itself with ugly precision. Anri stood near the classroom side-door in stockinged feet, caught between surprise and the beginnings of protest. Curtis held the open Nike box at a practical angle, not presenting it like a gift so much as completing an errand: dark, subtle, the right size, nothing flashy enough to turn the hallway into a spectacle. He was mid-sentence when Mikado appeared in the frame.
“They should fit. You shouldn’t have to walk around like that.”
Then Mikado was there, ruined pair hanging from his hand.
Anri saw him first. The conflict hit her face immediately — gratitude, embarrassment, the awareness that this looked like more than shoes.
“Ryuugamine-kun—” She turned to him before anyone else, voice quick and sincere. “Thank you. You actually found them.”
It was the right order. It did not save the image.
Curtis looked from Anri to the wrecked pair in Mikado’s grip. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he took in the soaked leather and split seam with the same flat practicality he brought to broken tools.
“…Yeah. Those are done.”
No mockery. That made it worse.
Mikado smiled too late.
The expression arrived a second after the room had already understood the hierarchy of objects: old pair recovered, new pair provided, only one of them able to leave the building on Anri’s feet. He stood there holding garbage and wearing the smile of a boy who had done the kind thing and still lost the function of it.
“That’s… good,” he said. “That you have something else.”
Anri’s eyes flicked between them, miserable in a quiet way. She accepted the new shoes because she needed them. She kept thanking Mikado because he had searched. Neither action repaired the doorway.
Masaomi, coming up the hall just in time to catch the end of it, took in the whole still life in one glance: ruined pair, new Nikes, Curtis already finished being useful, Mikado smiling like the floor had moved and he was pretending it hadn’t. His own smile flickered, then locked back into place too late as well.
That was when Shinra appeared, because of course he did, bright as a man collecting interesting specimens from a hallway that did not know it was about to donate one.
“Curtis! Perfect. I was hoping I’d catch you before you vanished into whatever nocturnal operating system you people run on.” He adjusted his glasses, delighted. “Dinner tonight. My place. Celty will be there. Mei too — old classmate, very sharp, you’ll like the contrast. Come. I insist. It’ll be good for the table.”
Curtis looked at the Nike box now in Anri’s hands, at Mikado still holding the ruined pair, at Shinra’s invitation sitting on top of a fracture he had not intended to cause.
“…Yeah,” he said, because he was still bad at closing doors. “Alright.”
Mikado heard that too.
The shoes had already been replaced.
Now the evening was being claimed as well.
And the boy with the ruined pair in his hands finally understood, without having language sharp enough for it yet, that finding what was lost and mattering were no longer the same thing.
Curtis had already started to turn after Shinra’s invitation, long black braids shifting against his uniform, the errand in his mind marked complete. The ruined pair was Mikado’s problem now. Dinner had become the next item on a day that would not stay small.
“Wait—Curtis-kun.”
Anri’s voice caught him before the hallway could.
He stopped. She took one step after him, new Nike box held against her like it weighed more than shoes, stockinged feet still on the floor because putting them on in front of both boys felt like choosing a side in a contest nobody had agreed to hold. Behind her, Mikado remained in the doorway frame with the wrecked pair hanging from his hand, smiling that late smile and pretending he was not listening.
Anri kept her voice low, aimed only at Curtis.
“…Thank you. For these. I mean that.” A short, strained pause as her eyes flicked once toward Mikado, then back. “But you didn’t have to run out and buy them. Ryuugamine-kun was already looking. I don’t want this to look like you thought his help didn’t count.”
Another quieter beat, more personal and harder to say.
“They’re… really nice. Too nice, maybe. I can pay you back.” She swallowed, fingers tightening on the box. “I just need you to know I wasn’t asking to be rescued again. Not like this. Not in front of everyone.”
She glanced down at the new pair, then up at his unrested face, caught between genuine gratitude and the fear that the doorway had already rewritten her into someone who kept being saved while another boy stood there holding what was left of the old version.
“…Please don’t make this a habit,” she finished, softer. “The replacing things before I can even stand in the loss for a second.”
The look that crossed Curtis’s face was not irritation.
It was defeat.
Long black braids shifted against his uniform as the errand collapsed in his hands from something practical into another version of going too far. He had seen a problem and closed it. He had not seen the doorway, the ruined pair, the late smile, or the way Anri would have to stand in the middle of being helped twice at once. The same expression from the fight aftermath flickered through him — that quiet recognition that his solution had arrived in the wrong shape.
“…I’m sorry.” His voice came out low, stripped of defense. “I won’t do it again.”
Then he began to walk away.
No argument. No explanation about sizes and walk-home logistics. No glance back at Shinra’s dinner still waiting on the other end of the afternoon. He simply took the correction, accepted the loss of the good intention, and put distance on it before the hallway could ask him to stand there looking like a man who had bought his way into a scene he never meant to occupy.
Anri remained behind with the new box in her arms and the apology still hanging in the air, the words I won’t do it again landing with more weight than she had asked for. She had wanted the replacing to stop. She had not wanted him to look like that while saying it.
Mikado watched Curtis’s back recede down the corridor, ruined shoes still in his own grip, and felt no victory in the retreat. If anything, the defeat on Curtis’s face only made the replacement sharper: even leaving, the other boy took the emotional center with him.
The new Nikes stayed in Anri’s hands.
The old pair stayed in Mikado’s.
And the boy who had paid for the difference was already gone before either of them could decide what the apology had actually cost.
The hallway had not even finished absorbing Curtis’s retreat when Rio spoke.
She had seen enough: the box, the ruined pair, the late smile, the defeated look on Curtis’s face, and Anri standing there holding new shoes like they were evidence of a crime. Rio’s voice came out low and sharp, not loud enough for the whole corridor, but far too exact to be mistaken for a passing comment.
“You’re really not good at talking to him.”
Anri turned.
Rio did not stop.
“You make him feel alienated, treating him the way you do. You call someone else -kun in front of him. Then you lecture him after he runs out of school to get you something nice.” Her eyes flicked once to Mikado, still holding the wrecked pair, then back to Anri. “And you still think Mikado is worth gushing over. Curtis-kun should stop worrying about you.”
The words hit the quiet girl like a slap made of accuracy and unfairness at the same time.
Anri’s face changed.
Not her usual withdrawn stillness. Not the careful guilt from the fight-stare. Actual anger — rare, bright, and immediate — rose through her so fast it startled even her. The Nike box tightened in her arms.
“Don’t you dare.”
The sentence came out sharper than her classmates had ever heard from her.
“You don’t get to stand there and reduce that to me gushing over someone, or to me trying to alienate him. I thanked Ryuugamine-kun because he searched all day. I asked Curtis-kun not to turn every broken thing in my life into something he has to replace before I can even breathe.” Her voice shook, but the anger held. “That is not cruelty. That is me trying not to become a person who only exists as another problem he has to solve.”
She took one step closer to Rio, dark eyes bright and wounded.
“And you don’t know what I think of him. You don’t know what yesterday cost. You don’t know what it looks like from my side when he disappears, then comes back, then spends money on me in a doorway while another person is still holding what he decided was trash.” A thinner, harsher breath. “If you’re going to defend him, defend him. Don’t rewrite me into someone who uses him and then lectures him for being kind.”
The hallway felt suddenly too small.
Mikado stood frozen with the ruined shoes.
Rio did not look away.
Anri, who almost never raised her voice for herself, was still standing in the aftershock of having done it — angry, exposed, and nowhere near finished feeling the cost of the afternoon.
Rio did not flinch from the rare anger. If anything, it made her finish the point instead of backing off.
“You can be trying not to become a problem and still make him feel like one,” she said. “Those two things happened in the same doorway.”
Anri’s mouth opened. Nothing useful came out.
Rio kept going, not louder, just tighter.
“He ran out of school, bought you something you could actually walk in, and the first thing he got back was a warning not to do it again. You thanked Mikado first, called him the familiar way, and then watched Curtis put on that defeated look and leave. That’s the part you’re not seeing. You think you’re being careful with him. From where he was standing, it looked like even a kindness has to apologize for existing if it comes from him.”
Anri’s grip on the Nike box went bloodless.
She wanted to say it was more complicated. That the fight-stare still sat in her chest. That Curtis disappearing had terrified her. That she had not meant the replacement to look like a verdict on Mikado. All of that was true. None of it unmade the image Rio was holding up: Curtis walking away sorry for helping, while she remained in the hallway with new shoes and an old pair still in someone else’s hands.
“You don’t have to gush over him,” Rio continued. “You don’t have to choose him in a hallway. But if you’re going to keep him close enough that he worries about you, then stop handing him the version of the room where he is useful only until he becomes too much. That look on his face just now? That’s what alienated looks like. He already thinks people stare at him like a freak when he goes too far. You just gave that feeling a quieter, more personal shape.”
Anri stood there, furious and unable to use the fury.
Because the worst part was not that Rio was being unkind.
It was that too much of it was right.
The thank-you order.
The lecture after the errand.
The way Ryuugamine-kun had come out so easily while Curtis absorbed I won’t do it again like a man putting himself back in a smaller box.
Anri’s anger stayed in her face, but her argument died in her throat. She looked down at the new Nikes, then toward the corridor Curtis had already emptied, and hated how clearly she could suddenly see the defeat she had put on him.
Rio exhaled once, still sharp, but finished.
“If you care about him at all, don’t wait until he’s gone to realize the way you talk to him matters. He will stop worrying about you if that’s what you keep teaching him to do. And you don’t get to act shocked when he believes the lesson.”
Rio slung her bag higher on her shoulder, already turning away from the wreckage of the doorway. She did not look at the new Nikes again. She did not look at the ruined pair in Mikado’s hands. Her last glance was for the empty stretch of corridor Curtis had used to leave.
“Maybe I will go see him tonight.” The words were plain, almost practical. “No one seems to be there for him. And he just seems to be there for everyone else.”
Then she walked off, leaving the sentence in the hallway like a second pair of shoes nobody had asked to hold.
Anri did not answer at full voice.
Her fist closed hard around the Nike box until the cardboard edge bit her fingers. The leftover anger, the humiliating accuracy of Rio’s lecture, and the image of Curtis’s defeated face all collided into one thin, involuntary line that barely made it past her teeth.
“…Stay away from him.”
It was not loud enough to stop Rio. It was not composed enough to sound like a reasonable request. It was the sound of a girl who had spent the last two days insisting she did not want to be rescued, then discovering she also could not stand the thought of someone else walking toward the same boy while she stood behind with gratitude, guilt, and no argument left that didn’t collapse on contact with the truth.
Mikado heard it.
That made it worse.
Anri kept her eyes on the box in her arms, jaw tight, the muttered claim already sitting in her chest like something she should not have said and would not take back. Rio’s footsteps faded. The new shoes remained. The old pair remained. And the boy they were all circling had already left school believing, once again, that the kindest thing he could do was disappear before he made the room more complicated.
Curtis was already a street away from Raira when the new phone started ringing.
Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he glanced down without stopping. Anri’s name sat on the screen with the same timing as everything else she had asked of him today: a beat too late to undo the doorway, a beat too soon for the defeat to have cooled. He kept walking, thumb hovering over the side of the phone, unrested eyes flat on the pavement ahead.
Do I really wanna answer this?
The thought arrived tired and unkind in a way he did not usually let himself be toward her. He could still see the look on her face when she told him not to make replacing things a habit. He could still hear himself saying he wouldn’t do it again. The Nike box. The ruined pair in Mikado’s hand. The late smile. All of it still sat in his chest like proof that showing up and solving a problem had only made him larger in a room that wanted him smaller.
The phone kept ringing.
Curtis’s mouth tightened.
She should just go hang with Mikado and Masaomi.
That was the easier map. The old map. The one where Anri stayed inside the shape she already knew, and he stopped mistaking practical care for a place he was allowed to occupy. He had dinner with Shinra later. He had a city already turning him into video. He had a night that did not need another careful conversation about how his kindness had landed wrong.
He stared at her name one second longer, thumb still uncommitted, the call buzzing against a palm that had already learned how quickly answering could become another way of staying too close.
The ringing stopped.
A second later the screen changed from incoming call to a message.
Curtis slowed despite himself, long black braids shifting against his uniform as Anri’s texts landed one after another, shorter than she probably wanted and still too full to be casual.
Anri:
Please don’t ignore this.
Anri:
I’m sorry. I didn’t mean for you to leave like that.
Anri:
The shoes are kind. I was grateful. I just handled it badly.
Anri:
I don’t want you to stop worrying because I said the wrong thing in a hallway.
A last one came through after a pause, as if she had fought herself over whether to send it.
Anri:
Can you answer? Even for a minute.
Curtis stopped at the edge of the next crossing, thumb resting against the glass. The afternoon crowd moved around him while the defeated look she had put on his face and the correction he had accepted both sat in the same hand as her apology. Mikado. Masaomi. The ruined pair. Rio’s absence from this particular screen. All of it pressed against a set of messages that were trying, too late and too honestly, to pull him back from the version of the day where he simply kept walking.
He stared at Can you answer? and hated that the smallest part of him already knew he would.
Curtis sighed at the crossing, long black braids shifting against his uniform as the last message sat there asking for even a minute.
“…I guess.”
He called her back.
Anri answered so fast it was obvious she had been holding the phone already, waiting on the chance he would not just keep walking.
“Curtis-kun—” His name came out tight with immediate relief, then snagged on everything she had not managed to say cleanly in the hallway. A short, unsteady breath filled the line. “Thank you. For calling. I thought you were going to leave it.”
Another pause, smaller and more careful now that she actually had him.
“…I saw your face when I said I didn’t want it to become a habit. I didn’t mean go away. I didn’t mean your help was a problem. I meant I was overwhelmed, and I put that on you in the worst possible order.” Her voice thinned. “I’m sorry. Please don’t decide from that doorway that you should just stay out of my life and leave me to Mikado and Masaomi.”
Curtis started walking again as he talked, long black braids shifting against his uniform, the crossing light already forgotten behind him. His voice came out flatter than her apology deserved, the old habit of making a wound sound logistical.
“It’s fine. I needed to go anyway.” A short, dry pause. “I guess Shinra would like to cut me open over dinner.”
The joke was thin on purpose. An exit with a medical undertone. A way to put a calendar item between himself and the doorway so the conversation would not have to stay inside the defeat she had just tried to take back.
Anri did not laugh.
“…Curtis-kun.” His name came out tighter this time, less relief and more the sound of someone hearing it’s fine for what it usually meant with him. “That isn’t funny. And it doesn’t tell me if we’re actually okay, or if you just needed a reason to get off the sidewalk before the conversation got any closer.”
A short breath.
“If you have dinner, go. I’m not trying to keep you in the street.” Her voice lowered. “I just don’t want ‘I needed to go anyway’ to be how you file what happened. I handled it badly. You looked… defeated. That stayed with me. Please don’t swallow the whole thing into a joke about someone cutting you open so I have to pretend the hallway didn’t matter.”
Another quieter beat, almost reluctant to ask and unable not to.
“…Where are you going? And are you coming back to school after, or is that you disappearing into the rest of the day again?”
Curtis kept walking, long black braids shifting against his uniform as the afternoon opened in front of him with no classroom left to justify turning around.
“I have no reason to come back to school today. I might just stay out.” A short pause, more honest than the Shinra joke. “A lot has been happening, and I don’t wanna be home alone.”
On the other end, Anri went still.
The words home alone landed directly on top of Rio’s voice in the hallway: No one seems to be there for him. And he just seems to be there for everyone else. Then the rest of it, worse because it had been aimed at her: Maybe I will go see him tonight. Anri’s fingers tightened around the phone. The new Nike box was still in her other arm. The ruined pair was still somewhere behind her in Mikado’s hands. And now Curtis was out in the city saying he did not want an empty room.
She hated that Rio had seen the gap first.
She hated more that the gap was real.
“…Then don’t be,” Anri said, quicker than her usual caution, the decision forming under the concern before she could dress it in better motives. “If you don’t want to go home alone, you don’t have to walk around until dinner by yourself either.”
A short breath. She could hear how it sounded. She sent it anyway.
“I can come meet you. Just for a little while. Before you go to Shinra’s.” The words came out careful and urgent at once, as if she were offering company and claiming a space Rio had already pointed at. “You bought me shoes and I sent you down the hall looking like that. If you’re going to stay out because a lot has been happening, then let me be there for part of it instead of leaving you to wander until someone else decides you look unattended.”
She swallowed, already moving before she could lose the nerve, the memory of stay away from him still hot in her mouth.
“…Tell me where you are, Curtis-kun. Please.”
Curtis stopped in the middle of the sidewalk so abruptly a cyclist had to swerve around him.
“…Tsk. The hell?”
The mutter was for himself. Anri’s sudden tell me where you are had come in too fast, too pointed, too much like someone moving to occupy a space he had only admitted was empty. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he stared at the phone and lined up the actual afternoon: gift before dinner, Shinra’s table, Celty, Mei, a room he was already walking into under-informed. He did not have time to be collected off the street like an unattended problem.
Then the softer calculation arrived anyway. She had called back. She had apologized. She was asking to show up. After the doorway, after I won’t do it again, slamming the door all the way shut felt uglier than rearranging the hour.
He lifted the phone.
“…I’m busy. I need to get a gift before I head to dinner.” A short pause as the rest left him more easily than a hard no would have. “Hmm. If you don’t mind, we can hang out after. Though I’m up late, if that’s okay with you, Anri-chan.”
On the other end, Anri’s eyes lit before she could stop them.
After.
Not Rio tonight filling the gap.
After.
The relief was real — he had not gone fully cold, he had used Anri-chan again, he had left her a place in the night. Under it sat a sharper, private satisfaction she would not have admitted out loud: she had gotten there first. Rio could talk about who was there for him. Anri already had a later hour with his name on it.
“…Yes,” she said, too quickly, then forced the happiness down into something that would not give her away. “That’s okay. Late is okay. I’ll wait until after your dinner.”
A smaller breath, warmer now that the claim had landed.
“Thank you. For not just saying you were busy and leaving it there.” She pressed the phone closer to her ear, already turning the rest of the afternoon into the space between now and after. “Text me when you’re done. I’ll come to you.”
She did not say I beat her.
She did not have to. The thought was already sitting in her chest, bright and unkind and tangled up with the part of her that had actually been afraid he would keep walking.
Curtis started walking again, long black braids shifting against his uniform as the practical piece of the afternoon reasserted itself over Anri’s sudden claim on the later hour.
“I’m being invited to dinner by two doctors. The least one should do is bring a gift.”
On the other end, Anri blinked, thrown by how ordinary the sentence sounded after a day of ruined shoes, late smiles, and defeated retreats. The boy who had just been a public fight video and a hallway fracture was now talking about host etiquette like it was the most obvious law in the city.
“…That’s… very polite of you,” she said, and meant it more than she expected to. A short pause, curiosity slipping in under the leftover tension. “Where did you even learn that? You don’t talk like someone who picked it up from this place.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged slightly.
“Books.”
No flourish. No story about parents or some charming mentor. Just the dry answer of a boy who had built pieces of himself out of pages because the people who should have taught him those things had not stayed around to do it.
Anri was quiet for a second, the single word rearranging him again in her head: the transfer student with insomnia, a street-fight myth already forming around him, and a habit of bringing gifts to doctors because a book had told him that was what a person did.
“…Books,” she repeated, softer. “That actually explains more than it should.”
Then, before the line could turn heavy again:
“Don’t pick anything strange. If they’re doctors, they’ll overthink it.”
Curtis’s tone thinned into something almost conversational as he cut toward the shops, long black braids shifting against his uniform.
“Shinra is a strange doctor. The strangest I’ve met by far.” A short breath, then the closer. “But anyway. I’ll let you go for now, Anri-chan. …Thanks for calling. I was just going to ignore the call, but…”
The pause lasted long enough to make the rest land cleaner than he probably meant it to.
“…I can’t ignore you.”
He hung up.
Anri remained in the corridor near the side door with the Nike box still in her arms and the dead line warm against her ear. For a second she only stood there, the afternoon noise of Raira moving around a girl who had started the call afraid she had pushed him into disappearing again.
I can’t ignore you.
It undid more of the doorway than the apology texts had. Not a confession. Not a claim. Just the plain admission that when her name came up on his phone, the version of him that wanted to keep walking could not quite do it. After Rio’s lecture, after stay away from him, after watching him leave with that defeated face, the sentence felt like proof she had not been written out.
She pressed the phone to her chest, dark eyes lowered, a small unguarded heat moving through her.
She still had a place in his heart.
Maybe not the only place. Maybe not a simple one.
But enough of one that he had called back when ignoring her would have been easier.
Anri let out a shaky breath, tightened her hold on the new shoes, and for the first time since the hallway fracture allowed herself the quiet, dangerous thought that the later hour he had given her was not only a schedule.
It was a door he had not closed.
Mikado had not gone far.
He was still near the side door with the ruined shoes hanging stupidly from one hand, late smile worn thin, when he saw Anri lower the phone from her ear like the call had left something warm and unsteadied in her face. The new Nike box was still in her arms. Curtis was already gone. That was the shape of the afternoon.
“Everything okay, Anri-chan?”
The old softness in it was automatic. Familiar. The version of her name he had been allowed to use when the group still felt like it belonged to the three of them.
Anri looked up, and for a second the answer almost came out in the old pattern. Then Rio’s voice cut through the reflex — you call someone else kun in front of him — and the word snagged before it could leave her mouth the way it always had.
“…I’m fine, Ryuugamine.”
No -kun.
It was small enough that a stranger might have missed it. Mikado did not. The missing honorific landed in the space between them like a shoe that had been moved one inch off its usual mark and therefore could not be ignored. Anri realized what she had done as soon as the name left her, color touching her face, but she did not correct it back. After the doorway, after Curtis’s defeated retreat, after I can’t ignore you still sitting in her chest, the old easy familiarity felt suddenly like a habit she had been using without seeing the weight of it.
“I just needed to talk to him,” she added, quieter, eyes dropping to the new box. “That’s all.”
Mikado stood there holding the ruined pair, hearing Ryuugamine where Ryuugamine-kun had always been, and felt the classroom map shift one more degree away from him. Anri had not shouted. She had not chosen anyone out loud. She had only taken a single familiar syllable away from him and left him to stand in the absence of it.
That absence was enough.
The afternoon split in two whether anyone agreed to it or not.

Lane A
Mikado did not go home.
He walked with the ruined shoes still in a plastic bag like evidence he could not throw away, Masaomi matching his pace with a brightness that kept thinning at the edges. Ikebukuro’s after-school noise tried to make the day ordinary again. It failed. Anri had said Ryuugamine instead of Ryuugamine-kun. Curtis had left with a dinner invitation and a defeated apology. The new Nikes were already on their way into Anri’s life. The old pair were only useful as a reminder that Mikado had arrived too late with the wrong object.
“You’re holding those like they insulted you,” Masaomi said.
Mikado’s late smile flickered on, then died.
“…I found them.”
“I know.” Masaomi’s voice dropped, the joke finally set down. “And he replaced them. Those are not the same victory.”
The sentence landed so cleanly that Mikado had no defense for it. He looked at the bag in his hand and understood, with a sick little precision, that he had spent the day recovering a loss the room no longer needed. The city had not punished him. It had simply moved the useful role to someone else.
They passed a girl with a sign for lost things and a notebook hanging from her neck. Masaomi would have kept walking. Mikado stopped. When the notebook was offered, he hesitated long enough to make the pause itself a confession, then wrote the thing he could not say to Anri in the hallway:
I want the version of this city where I was still necessary.
He closed the book before Masaomi could read it.
Behind them, the social fracture kept widening on its own: Anri waiting on an after dinner text, Rio already considering a night visit, Curtis somewhere in the city buying a gift for doctors instead of coming back to class. Mikado felt all of it without having the facts, which made the jealousy worse. He did not need the facts. He had the ruined shoes.

Lane B
Curtis bought the gift first.
Nothing extravagant — a clean boxed set of good tea, the kind a book would call appropriate for a household of strange professionals who already had too many sharp objects and not enough ordinary gestures. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he started toward Shinra’s with the bag in one hand and Anri’s later hour sitting unanswered in the other.
He never made it inside.
The street near Yagiri Pharmaceuticals broke first: a sharp string of firecracker bursts, shouting, the ugly little panic of a public disturbance that was not quite a bombing and not quite a joke. People turned. A security line moved. Somewhere in the noise a young man was screaming about a missing girl and a company that took things it should not have touched.
Curtis stopped at the edge of it, unrested eyes narrowing, already reading the scene as more than random street theater. Before he could step closer, his new phone lit with Shinra’s name.
“Change of venue!” Shinra sounded far too lively for a man being pulled toward a lab incident. “Something uncivil is happening at Yagiri and I am apparently required. Don’t go to the apartment. Russia Sushi. I’ll bring Celty. Mei too, if she doesn’t vanish out of spite. Same dinner, fewer firecrackers in the soup. Be there.”
The call ended before Curtis could ask whether this counted as the cut-him-open evening or a postponement of it.
He stood in the settling noise with a gift bag and a rerouted night. Russia Sushi. Celty. Mei. The first meeting of the two originals now moving from a private table to a public counter, which would make the reading harder and the fortune later possible. He exhaled once through his nose and turned off the direct route to Shinra’s building.
Fine.
Different room.
Same test.
Across the ward, Kadota was already watching the pharmaceutical building with the look of a man who had filed too many rumors to believe this was only firecrackers. The Chief of the North video sat in that filing now too, whether Curtis wanted it there or not.
Curtis texted no one yet. Not Anri. Not Rio. Not Celty.
He headed for Russia Sushi with the tea bag in hand, unaware that Mikado was still walking around with a ruined pair of shoes and a sentence in a stranger’s notebook, and that the dinner he thought had merely changed restaurants was about to put him across from Mei for the first time while Celty watched both of them over a plate he had not ordered.
